{"abstract":"Stack Overflow is one of the most influential Software Question & Answer (SQA) websites, hosting millions of programming-related questions and answers. Tags play a critical role in efficiently organizing the contents on Stack Overflow and are vital to support various site operations, such as querying relevant content. Poorly chosen tags often lead to issues such as tag ambiguity and tag explosion. Therefore, a precise and accurate automated tag recommendation technique is needed. Inspired by the recent success of pre-trained models (PTMs) in natural language processing (NLP), we present PTM4Tag+, a tag recommendation framework for Stack Overflow posts that utilize PTMs in language modeling. PTM4Tag+ is implemented with a triplet architecture, which considers three key components of a post, i.e., Title, Description, and Code, with independent PTMs. We utilize a number of popular pre-trained models, including BERT-based models (e.g., BERT, RoBERTa, CodeBERT, BERTOverflow, and ALBERT), and encoder-decoder models (e.g., PLBART, CoTexT, and CodeT5). Our results show that leveraging CodeT5 under the PTM4Tag+ framework achieves the best performance among the eight considered PTMs and outperforms the state-of-the-art Convolutional Neural Network-based approach by a substantial margin in terms of average Precision@k, Recall@k, and F1-score@k (k ranges from 1 to 5). Specifically, CodeT5 improves the performance of F1-score@1-5 by 8.8%, 12.4%, 15.3%, 16.4%, and 16.6%, respectively. Moreover, to address the concern with inference latency, we experimented PTM4Tag+ using smaller PTM models (i.e., DistilBERT, DistilRoBERTa, CodeBERT-small, and CodeT5-small). We find that although smaller PTMs cannot outperform larger PTMs, they still maintain over 93.96% of the performance on average while reducing the mean inference time by more than 47.2%.","author_count":8,"contributors":["He, Junda","Xu, Bowen","Yang, Zhou","Han, Dong Gyun","Yang, Chengran","Liu, Jiakun","Zhao, Zhipeng","Lo, David"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-790387"],"filters":{"added":"2025-02","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"6-10 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["America, North","Asia, South","Europe, Non-EU","OECD"],"country":["Singapore","United Kingdom","United States"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","North Carolina State University","Royal Holloway University of London","Singapore Management University"],"keyword":["Pre trained models","Tag recommendation","Transformer"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-KU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-KU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"i ptm000000000004tag i tag recommendation of stack overflow posts with pre trained models","src":["ku"],"src-code":["DI-U-KU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"loc","url":"https://curis.ku.dk/ws/files/413055204/PTM4Tag_.pdf","version":"Pre-print"},"id":"S01-000.00-790387","identifier":[{"id":"85209697012","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["ku-63bcdc96-c3d5-4e5a-b6db-d8ac8567cffe"],"keyword":["Pre-trained models","Stack overflow","Tag recommendation","Transformer"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-A0BCCCC5365FBCC762A3FA9C0CD4A4DA"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"loc","url":"https://curis.ku.dk/ws/files/413055204/PTM4Tag_.pdf","version":"Pre-print"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","country":"sg","key":"singapore management university","loc":["Singapore","Asia, South"],"name":"Singapore Management University","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"us","key":"north carolina state university","loc":["United States","America, North","OECD"],"name":"North Carolina State University","used":1},{"affno":"3","country":"gb","key":"royal holloway university of london","loc":["United Kingdom","Europe, Non-EU","OECD"],"name":"Royal Holloway University of London","used":1},{"affno":"4","key":"ku university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-KU","l3nl":"University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"KU University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"KU University of Copenhagen","namevar":["Københavns Universitet, Faculty of Science, Department of Computer Science"],"nora_code":"DI-U-KU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","first":"Junda","key":"hejundapau","last":"He","name":"He, Junda","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","first":"Bowen","key":"xubowenpau","last":"Xu","name":"Xu, Bowen","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Zhou","key":"yangzhoupau","last":"Yang","name":"Yang, Zhou","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"3","first":"Dong Gyun","key":"handonggyunpau","last":"Han","name":"Han, Dong Gyun","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Chengran","key":"yangchengranpau","last":"Yang","name":"Yang, Chengran","rank":5,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Jiakun","key":"liujiakunpau","last":"Liu","name":"Liu, Jiakun","rank":6,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"4","country":"dk","first":"Zhipeng","key":"zhaozhipengpau","last":"Zhao","locid":"e5c18578-ae38-4581-87a8-8093763d3b75","name":"Zhao, Zhipeng","rank":7,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"David","key":"lodavidpau","last":"Lo","name":"Lo, David","rank":8,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1007/s10664-024-10576-z","issn":["13823256","15737616"],"issn_org":["13823256"],"issue":"1","journal":"Empirical Software Engineering","paperid":"28","status":"p","title":"Empirical Software Engineering","volume":"30"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85209697012"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Universities","University of Copenhagen"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["ku-63bcdc96-c3d5-4e5a-b6db-d8ac8567cffe"],"src":["KU University of Copenhagen"],"title":"<i>PTM4Tag</i>+ : Tag recommendation of stack overflow posts with pre-trained models","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-790387","year":"2025","yearsub":"2025"}
{"abstract":"Phage therapy has emerged as a highly effective antibiotic alternative for treating Salmonella infections; however, research into how phage dosage, administration timing, and frequency influence therapeutic efficacy—along with phage pharmacokinetics—remains limited. In this study, we established a Salmonella Pullorum infection model in specific-pathogen-free (SPF) chickens to investigate these parameters, alongside the therapeutic efficacy and pharmacokinetic distribution of the phage. The study consisted of two sequential experiments: trial 1 systematically evaluated the effects of phage dosage, administration timing, and frequency on treatment outcomes, leading to the identification of an optimal dosing regimen. This optimized regimen was then applied in trial 2, which focused on characterizing the pharmacodynamic and pharmacokinetic profiles of phage vB_SalS_JNS02 in SPF chickens. The results demonstrated that phage dosage, administration timing, and frequency significantly influenced survival rates: prophylactic administration was more effective than delayed treatment, and notably, higher doses did not consistently yield superior outcomes when administered at the same time point. Phage treatment effectively reduced Salmonella colonization in the blood, tissues, and intestines, achieving complete clearance of the pathogen from the brain and blood within 2–3 days post-challenge, and from the heart and ileum by day 5. Furthermore, phage therapy restored intestinal morphological parameters—including villus height and crypt depth—to levels comparable with those of uninfected controls, while simultaneously mitigating Salmonella-induced histopathological damage and promoting tissue repair. Interestingly, although phage administration altered the composition and abundance of specific gut microbiota taxa, it exerted no significant impact on alpha diversity. Pharmacokinetic analysis revealed that orally administered phages rapidly entered the systemic circulation and tissues, reaching peak concentrations at 4 hours post-administration. Phages were completely cleared from the blood by day 5 but remained detectable in the lungs, kidneys, spleen, and liver until the final observation time point (day 9). Collectively, these findings indicate that phage JNS02 exhibits favorable pharmacokinetic and pharmacodynamics properties, positioning it as a promising antibiotic alternative and a candidate for clinical use in treating Salmonella infections.","author_count":13,"contributors":["Li, Lulu","Zhang, Mengrui","Cai, Yumei","Liu, Zhengjie","Tang, Zhaohui","Zhang, Zhipeng","Liu, Yujia","Zhao, Caihong","Yu, Xiaoqing","Petersen, Bent","Sicheritz-Pontén, Thomas","Clokie, Martha R. J.","Liu, Yuqing"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-829711"],"filters":{"added":"2025-10","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"11-50 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["Asia, East","Europe, Non-EU","OECD"],"country":["China","United Kingdom"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","Shandong Academy of Agricultural Sciences","Shandong Agricultural University","Sino-Danish Center for education and research","University of Leicester"],"keyword":["Phage therapy","Pharmacodynamic","Pharmacokinetic"],"level":"sci","mra":["med"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-KU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-KU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"optimizing phage therapy for i salmonella i pullorum dosage timing and pharmacokinetics define treatment efficacy in spf chickens","src":["ku"],"src-code":["DI-U-KU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC-ND","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.psj.2025.105643","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-829711","identifier":[{"id":"40848485","type":"pmid"},{"id":"105013869524","type":"scopus"},{"id":"PMC12398828","type":"pmcid"}],"ids":["ku-b7409592-038e-4c9f-8f0d-1db0f5af2d3e"],"keyword":["Phage therapy","Pharmacodynamic","Pharmacokinetic","Salmonella Pullorum"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Medical Science"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-2-0C783FBA92CE3650021252ED798D578D"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC-ND","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.psj.2025.105643","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC-ND","type":"loc","url":"https://curis.ku.dk/ws/files/462578894/1-s2.0-S0032579125008843-main.pdf","version":"Published Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","country":"cn","key":"shandong academy of agricultural sciences","loc":["China","Asia, East"],"name":"Shandong Academy of Agricultural Sciences","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"cn","key":"sino danish center for education and research","loc":["China","Asia, East"],"name":"Sino-Danish Center for education and research","used":1},{"affno":"3","country":"cn","key":"shandong agricultural university","loc":["China","Asia, East"],"name":"Shandong Agricultural University","used":1},{"affno":"4","country":"gb","key":"university of leicester","loc":["United Kingdom","Europe, Non-EU","OECD"],"name":"University of Leicester","used":1},{"affno":"5","key":"ku university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-KU","l3nl":"University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"KU University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"KU University of Copenhagen","namevar":["Københavns Universitet, Faculty of Health and Medical Sciences, Globe Institute, Section for Hologenomics"],"nora_code":"DI-U-KU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1 2","first":"Lulu","key":"lilulupau","last":"Li","name":"Li, Lulu","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1 2 3","first":"Mengrui","key":"zhangmengruipau","last":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Mengrui","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"3","first":"Yumei","key":"caiyumeipau","last":"Cai","name":"Cai, Yumei","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2 4","first":"Zhengjie","key":"liuzhengjiepau","last":"Liu","name":"Liu, Zhengjie","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1 2","first":"Zhaohui","key":"tangzhaohuipau","last":"Tang","name":"Tang, Zhaohui","rank":5,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1 2 3","first":"Zhipeng","key":"zhangzhipengpau","last":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Zhipeng","rank":6,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1 2 3","first":"Yujia","key":"liuyujiapau","last":"Liu","name":"Liu, Yujia","rank":7,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1 2 3","first":"Caihong","key":"zhaocaihongpau","last":"Zhao","name":"Zhao, Caihong","rank":8,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1 2","first":"Xiaoqing","key":"yuxiaoqingpau","last":"Yu","name":"Yu, Xiaoqing","rank":9,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2 5","first":"Bent","key":"petersenbentpau","last":"Petersen","locid":"e94fb81b-b165-42a8-8011-11eea5c95323","name":"Petersen, Bent","orcid":"0000-0002-2472-8317","rank":10,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2 5","first":"Thomas","key":"sicheritzponténthomaspau","last":"Sicheritz-Pontén","locid":"1a7c94b9-a434-4d46-80cb-926438326f7f","name":"Sicheritz-Pontén, Thomas","orcid":"0000-0001-6615-1141","rank":11,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2 4","first":"Martha R. J.","key":"clokiemartharjpau","last":"Clokie","name":"Clokie, Martha R. J.","rank":12,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1 2","first":"Yuqing","key":"liuyuqingpau","last":"Liu","name":"Liu, Yuqing","rank":13,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pmid":["40848485"],"pub":{"doi":"10.1016/j.psj.2025.105643","issn":["00325791","15253171"],"issn_org":["00325791"],"issue":"11","journal":"Poultry Science","paperid":"105643","status":"p","title":"Poultry Science","volume":"104"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-105013869524"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Universities","University of Copenhagen"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["ku-b7409592-038e-4c9f-8f0d-1db0f5af2d3e"],"src":["KU University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Optimizing phage therapy for <i>Salmonella</i> Pullorum : dosage, timing, and pharmacokinetics define treatment efficacy in SPF chickens","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-829711","year":"2025","yearsub":"2025"}
{"abstract":"Background: Zoonotic viruses cause substantial public health and socioeconomic problems worldwide. Understanding how viruses evolve and spread within and among wildlife species is a critical step when aiming for proactive identification of viral threats to prevent future pandemics. Despite the many proposed factors influencing viral diversity, the genomic diversity and structure of viral communities in East Africa are largely unknown. Results: Using 38.3 Tb of metatranscriptomic data obtained via ultradeep sequencing, we screened vertebrate-associated viromes from 844 bats and 250 rodents from Kenya and Uganda collected from the wild. The 251 vertebrate-associated viral genomes of bats (212) and rodents (39) revealed the vast diversity, host-related variability, and high geographic specificity of viruses in East Africa. Among the surveyed viral families, Coronaviridae and Circoviridae showed low host specificity, high conservation of replication-associated proteins, high divergence among viral entry proteins, and frequent recombination. Despite major dispersal limitations, recurrent mutations, cocirculation, and occasional gene flow contribute to the high local diversity of viral genomes. Conclusions: The present study not only shows the landscape of bat and rodent viromes in this zoonotic hotspot but also reveals genomic signatures driven by the evolution and dispersal of the viral community, laying solid groundwork for future proactive surveillance of emerging zoonotic pathogens in wildlife. F1ZzYbc5Jf4XpNJGrZx-82 Video Abstract","author_count":29,"contributors":["Wang, Daxi","Yang, Xinglou","Ren, Zirui","Hu, Ben","Zhao, Hailong","Yang, Kaixin","Shi, Peibo","Zhang, Zhipeng","Feng, Qikai","Nawenja, Carol Vannesa","Obanda, Vincent","Robert, Kityo","Nalikka, Betty","Waruhiu, Cecilia Njeri","Ochola, Griphin Ochieng","Onyuok, Samson Omondi","Ochieng, Harold","Li, Bei","Zhu, Yan","Si, Haorui","Yin, Jiefang","Kristiansen, Karsten","Jin, Xin","Xu, Xun","Xiao, Minfeng","Agwanda, Bernard","Ommeh, Sheila","Li, Junhua","Shi, Zheng Li"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-730634"],"filters":{"added":"2024-05","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"11-50 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["Africa","Asia, East","OECD","Oceania"],"country":["Australia","China","Kenya","Uganda"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","BGI","CAS - Wuhan Institute of Virology","Chinese Academy of Sciences","Hubei Jiangxia Laboratory","Kenya Wildlife Service","Makerere University","National Museums of Kenya","University of Chinese Academy of Sciences","University of Queensland"],"keyword":["Evolution","Metatranscriptome","Virome"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-KU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-KU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"substantial viral diversity in bats and rodents from east africa insights into evolution recombination and cocirculation","src":["ku"],"src-code":["DI-U-KU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1186/s40168-024-01782-4","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-730634","identifier":[{"id":"PMC11005217","type":"pmcid"},{"id":"85189888408","type":"scopus"},{"id":"38600530","type":"pmid"}],"ids":["ku-23e461ae-9374-4394-8a1b-a5839f5033e5"],"keyword":["Evolution","Metatranscriptome","Viral surveillance","Virome"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-DE7B887668D01D3198391F01C0A7ACAA"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1186/s40168-024-01782-4","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"loc","url":"https://curis.ku.dk/ws/files/388830040/s40168_024_01782_4.pdf","version":"Published Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","country":"cn","key":"bgi","loc":["China","Asia, East"],"name":"BGI","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"c